# Development workflow (functionality work)

**Canonical sources:** `practices/project_practices.md` and the skill `.cursor/skills/cad-feature-workflow/SKILL.md` (Stage 1 Understand, Stage 2 Implementation checklist, Stage 3 After implementation). There's no separate implementation-log journal in this project — `git commit` messages are the record.

## When this applies

Use the full three-stage workflow when the task **changes behavior or code** under the product tree: new feature, bugfix, behavior tweak, or non-trivial refactor in `src/`, `include/`, `CMakeLists.txt`, or shaders/assets the app loads.

## When to skip or lighten it

- **Read-only:** explaining code, architecture questions, “what does X do?” with no edits.
- **Explicit opt-out:** the user says e.g. no commit, quick fix only.

## What to do (short checklist)

1. **Stage 1:** Confirm problem/approach before touching code — see the skill.
2. **Stage 2:** Implement using the checklist in the skill (critique, architecture, performance, portability, naming, consistency, DRY, post-implementation diff read).
3. **Stage 3 before treating work as done:** clean build → quick Stage-2 pass as review → **`git commit`** with a **detailed** message (problem, approach, main files, follow-ups) unless the user asked not to commit.

If the change fails mid-flight, return to Stage 1 and question whether the original approach was correct.

## Git

Project owner prefers **rich, detailed commit history**. When completing applicable work, stage relevant files and commit; do not leave the session “done” with only uncommitted changes unless the user declined a commit or the environment cannot grant git access.
